        ('        },\n        "earth_orbital_zones": {\n          "leo_inner": {\n            "name": "Low Earth Orbit, inner edge (200 km)",\n            "description": "The lower edge of low Earth orbit, the band of space where most satellites and the Space Station fly.",\n            "about": "Low Earth orbit is the region from roughly 200 to 2,000 km up. Satellites there circle at every angle to the equator, so together they form a shell around Earth rather than a ring, and each completes an orbit in about 90 to 120 minutes. The International Space Station, the Hubble Space Telescope, Starlink and most weather and Earth-observing satellites live here; the moving lights seen at dusk and dawn are usually these.",\n            "radius": {\n              "value": 1.03135712,\n              "unit": "r_earth",',
         '        },\n        "earth_orbital_zones": {\n          "leo_inner": {\n            "name": "Low Earth Orbit, inner edge (180 km)",\n            "description": "The lower edge of low Earth orbit, the band of space where most satellites and the Space Station fly.",\n            "about": "Low Earth orbit is the region from roughly 180 to 2,000 km up. Satellites there circle at every angle to the equator, so together they form a shell around Earth rather than a ring, and each completes an orbit in about 90 to 120 minutes. The International Space Station, the Hubble Space Telescope, Starlink and most weather and Earth-observing satellites live here; the moving lights seen at dusk and dawn are usually these.",\n            "radius": {\n              "value": 1.03135712,\n              "unit": "r_earth",'),
        ('            "n_points": 30,\n            "marker_size": 2.5,\n            "source": "IERS Conventions (2010), Petit & Luzum (eds.), IERS Technical Note No. 36, Table 1.1; IAU B3 rounds to 6378.1 km",\n            "source_note": "Drawing floor: the LEO region begins at the surface (IADC-02-01 Rev. 3); 200 km marks where orbits stop decaying within days. Declared, not measured.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_LEO_INNER_KM"\n          },\n          "leo_outer": {\n            "name": "Low Earth Orbit, outer edge (2,000 km)",\n            "description": "The upper edge of low Earth orbit, about 2,000 km up, above which the sky belongs to higher orbits.",\n            "about": "Low Earth orbit is the region from roughly 200 to 2,000 km up. Satellites there circle at every angle to the equator, so together they form a shell around Earth rather than a ring, and each completes an orbit in about 90 to 120 minutes. The International Space Station, the Hubble Space Telescope, Starlink and most weather and Earth-observing satellites live here; the moving lights seen at dusk and dawn are usually these.",\n            "radius": {\n              "value": 1.31357121,\n              "unit": "r_earth",',
         '            "n_points": 30,\n            "marker_size": 2.5,\n            "source": "IERS Conventions (2010), Petit & Luzum (eds.), IERS Technical Note No. 36, Table 1.1; IAU B3 rounds to 6378.1 km",\n            "source_note": "The region itself begins at the surface; the inner edge is drawn at the lowest height satellites fly.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_LEO_INNER_KM"\n          },\n          "leo_outer": {\n            "name": "Low Earth Orbit, outer edge (2,000 km)",\n            "description": "The upper edge of low Earth orbit, about 2,000 km up, above which the sky belongs to higher orbits.",\n            "about": "Low Earth orbit is the region from roughly 180 to 2,000 km up. Satellites there circle at every angle to the equator, so together they form a shell around Earth rather than a ring, and each completes an orbit in about 90 to 120 minutes. The International Space Station, the Hubble Space Telescope, Starlink and most weather and Earth-observing satellites live here; the moving lights seen at dusk and dawn are usually these.",\n            "radius": {\n              "value": 1.31357121,\n              "unit": "r_earth",'),
        ('            "n_points": 120,\n            "marker_size": 2.0,\n            "source": "IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, Table 1.1 -- geocentric gravitational constant GM_E = 3.986004418e14 m^3 s^-2 (uncertainty 8e5 m^3 s^-2). Converted to km^3 s^-2 here.; IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, Table 1.2 \\"Parameters of the Geodetic Reference System GRS80\\" -- nominal mean Earth angular velocity, 7.292115e-5 rad s^-1. Corrected 2026-09-19: the old line said Table 1.1, which has no angular velocity in it. The value is unchanged; only the table is.",\n            "source_note": "Derived: (GM_E / omega^2)^(1/3) from IERS Conventions (2010) TN36 Table 1.1 -- GM_E = 3.986004418e14 m^3 s^-2, nominal mean angular velocity 7.292115e-5 rad s^-1. The hover\'s sentence on keeping pace: NASA Earth Observatory, Riebeek (2009, page updated 2025-11-14), Catalog of Earth Satellite Orbits, section High Earth Orbit, https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/catalog-of-earth-satellite-orbits/ -- at 42,164 km from Earth\'s centre the orbit matches Earth\'s rotation, so a satellite seems to stay over a single longitude; circular and over the equator, it is geostationary, always over the same place (read 2026-10-06).",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_GEOSTATIONARY_RADIUS_KM",\n            "note": "A ring in the equatorial plane, not a sphere, tilted with Earth\'s equator by the served pole. 42,164 km from Earth\'s centre, 35,786 km up."\n          },',
         '            "n_points": 120,\n            "marker_size": 2.0,\n            "source": "IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, Table 1.1 -- geocentric gravitational constant GM_E = 3.986004418e14 m^3 s^-2 (uncertainty 8e5 m^3 s^-2). Converted to km^3 s^-2 here.; IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, Table 1.2 \\"Parameters of the Geodetic Reference System GRS80\\" -- nominal mean Earth angular velocity, 7.292115e-5 rad s^-1. Corrected 2026-09-19: the old line said Table 1.1, which has no angular velocity in it. The value is unchanged; only the table is.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_GEOSTATIONARY_RADIUS_KM",\n            "note": "A ring in the equatorial plane, not a sphere, tilted with Earth\'s equator by the served pole. 42,164 km from Earth\'s centre, 35,786 km up."\n          },'),
