        ('            "about": "The solar wind drags Earth\'s magnetic field out behind the planet into a long tail. Spacecraft crossing it far downstream found that it stops widening about 120 Earth radii behind Earth and is about 60 Earth radii across beyond there. ISEE-3 followed it out to 220 Earth radii, which is how far the spacecraft went rather than where the tail ends. The tail is drawn round, which is close to its shape on average; at any moment it is often flattened, in a direction set by the solar wind\'s own magnetic field, which keeps changing.",\n            "_declared": "The tail continues Shue\'s magnetopause surface from where the drawing of that surface stops, at the magnetopause\'s served cut angle; its starting distance and radius are worked out in the page from the served Shue rows, not served. From there it widens in a straight line to drawn_radius at flare_end, then keeps that radius to drawn_end, and it is round. flare_end and diameter are measurements; drawn_radius and drawn_end are rules over them, declared on their rows in constants_new.py, and the page uses them only for the shape. The five numbers the orrery\'s tail was drawn with until 2026-09-25 (100, 15 and 25 Earth radii, colour and opacity), which nothing in the gallery read, are gone (L-322 Stage D, gallery patch 3). color and opacity here are drawing choices, the magnetopause\'s, so the two read as one surface.",\n            "flare_end": {\n              "value": 120.0,\n              "unit": "r_earth",\n              "in": {"au": {"value": 0.005, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 800000.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 2e-08, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 120.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 1.1, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},',
         '            "about": "The solar wind drags Earth\'s magnetic field out behind the planet into a long tail. Spacecraft crossing it far downstream found that it stops widening about 120 Earth radii behind Earth and is about 60 Earth radii across beyond there. ISEE-3 followed it out to 220 Earth radii, which is how far the spacecraft went rather than where the tail ends. The tail is drawn round, which is close to its shape on average; at any moment it is often flattened, in a direction set by the solar wind\'s own magnetic field, which keeps changing.",\n            "_declared": "The tail continues Shue\'s magnetopause surface from where the drawing of that surface stops, at the magnetopause\'s served cut angle; its starting distance and radius are worked out in the page from the served Shue rows, not served. From there it widens in a straight line to drawn_radius at flare_end, then keeps that radius to drawn_end, and it is round. flare_end and diameter are measurements; drawn_radius and drawn_end are rules over them, declared on their rows in constants_new.py, and the page uses them only for the shape. The five numbers the orrery\'s tail was drawn with until 2026-09-25 (100, 15 and 25 Earth radii, colour and opacity), which nothing in the gallery read, are gone (L-322 Stage D, gallery patch 3). color and opacity here are drawing choices, the magnetopause\'s, so the two read as one surface.",\n            "flare_end": {\n              "hover_line": "Spacecraft found the tail stops widening about {flare} Earth radii behind Earth, plus or minus {flare_unc}, and is about {width} Earth radii wide beyond there, plus or minus {width_unc}.",\n              "value": 120.0,\n              "unit": "r_earth",\n              "in": {"au": {"value": 0.005, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 800000.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 2e-08, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 120.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 1.1, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},'),
        ('              "in": {"au": {"value": 0.009, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 1400000.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 4.5e-08, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 220.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 2.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},\n              "figures": 2,\n              "source": "Slavin, J. A., Tsurutani, B. T., Smith, E. J., Jones, D. E. and Sibeck, D. G. (1983), \\"Average configuration of the distant (less than 220-earth-radii) magnetotail - Initial ISEE-3 magnetic field results\\", Geophys. Res. Lett. 10(10), 973-976, doi:10.1029/GL010i010p00973 -- the abstract states that the magnetotail retains much of its near-Earth structure out to X = -220 Earth radii, that flaring ceases at 100-120 R_E, and that the tail diameter settles near 60 R_E.",\n              "source_note": "the abstract states that the magnetotail retains much of its near-Earth structure out to X = -220 Earth radii. Abstract open at ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19830066648. The same abstract\'s figures for where flaring ends and for the tail\'s diameter are superseded by the same mission\'s 1985 averages, served here as flare_end and diameter.",\n              "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_MAGNETOTAIL_OBSERVED_RADII",\n              "note": "Read as OBSERVED TO AT LEAST, not as an edge. 220 R_E is ISEE-3\'s reach on its first two tail passes, so the figure is bounded by the orbit rather than by where the tail ends. Two further signatures are more distant and less like a tail: Ness, Scearce and Cantarano (1967), doi:10.1029/JZ072i015p03769, report Pioneer 7 in the expected tail region at 900 to 1,050 R_E over a week in 1966, intermittently and with no coherent tail, the title saying \'probable\'; and Intriligator et al. (1979), Geophys. Res. Lett. 6:585, report tail-ASSOCIATED phenomena near 3,100 R_E, read by later work as signatures disconnected from Earth."\n            },',
         '              "in": {"au": {"value": 0.009, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 1400000.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 4.5e-08, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 220.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 2.0, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},\n              "figures": 2,\n              "source": "Slavin, J. A., Tsurutani, B. T., Smith, E. J., Jones, D. E. and Sibeck, D. G. (1983), \\"Average configuration of the distant (less than 220-earth-radii) magnetotail - Initial ISEE-3 magnetic field results\\", Geophys. Res. Lett. 10(10), 973-976, doi:10.1029/GL010i010p00973 -- the abstract states that the magnetotail retains much of its near-Earth structure out to X = -220 Earth radii, that flaring ceases at 100-120 R_E, and that the tail diameter settles near 60 R_E.",\n              "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_MAGNETOTAIL_OBSERVED_RADII",\n              "note": "Read as OBSERVED TO AT LEAST, not as an edge. 220 R_E is ISEE-3\'s reach on its first two tail passes, so the figure is bounded by the orbit rather than by where the tail ends. Two further signatures are more distant and less like a tail: Ness, Scearce and Cantarano (1967), doi:10.1029/JZ072i015p03769, report Pioneer 7 in the expected tail region at 900 to 1,050 R_E over a week in 1966, intermittently and with no coherent tail, the title saying \'probable\'; and Intriligator et al. (1979), Geophys. Res. Lett. 6:585, report tail-ASSOCIATED phenomena near 3,100 R_E, read by later work as signatures disconnected from Earth."\n            },'),
        ('            "unit": "deg",\n            "figures": 5,\n            "source": "Alken et al. (2021), \\"International Geomagnetic Reference Field: the thirteenth generation\\", Earth Planets Space 73:49, doi:10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x -- for the RELATION: the geomagnetic poles are computed from the three degree-1 Gauss coefficients, which is what this row does. The paper prints no tilt of its own. Its Table 4 gives the 2020.0 north geomagnetic pole at 80.65 N, -72.68 E; these coefficients reproduce it as 80.6512 N, -72.6797 E (read 2026-09-21 by Claude Opus 5).",\n            "source_note": "the angle between the geomagnetic dipole axis and Earth\'s rotation axis at epoch 2020.0. The paper prints no tilt of its own: constants_new.py computes it from the paper\'s three degree-1 Gauss coefficients, the relation the paper gives for the geomagnetic poles, and serves it at five figures. It is decreasing by about 0.049 degrees a year (magnetic_tilt_rate, from the same coefficients\' published rates), so any quotation of it carries its epoch. The belts\' hover says the magnetic equator turns with Earth: the paper\'s Table 4 places the 2020.0 north geomagnetic pole at a point on Earth, 80.65 N, -72.68 E (constants_new.py, read 2026-09-21), so the dipole turns with the ground beneath it. This sentence said \'rounded to a tenth of a degree\' and \'about 0.05 deg per decade\' until 2026-09-26; the decade figure was ten times too small (L-322 Stage D, gallery patch 3).",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_DIPOLE_TILT_DEG",\n            "note": "Served so the hover can quote it. The rings are NOT tilted by it. They are drawn in the equatorial plane, which is the daily average of the magnetic one, because a tilted plane needs a direction as well as an angle and that direction turns once a day while this scene is frozen (L-231, Tony\'s ruling 2026-09-15)."\n          },',
         '            "unit": "deg",\n            "figures": 5,\n            "source": "Alken et al. (2021), \\"International Geomagnetic Reference Field: the thirteenth generation\\", Earth Planets Space 73:49, doi:10.1186/s40623-020-01288-x -- for the RELATION: the geomagnetic poles are computed from the three degree-1 Gauss coefficients, which is what this row does. The paper prints no tilt of its own. Its Table 4 gives the 2020.0 north geomagnetic pole at 80.65 N, -72.68 E; these coefficients reproduce it as 80.6512 N, -72.6797 E (read 2026-09-21 by Claude Opus 5).",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_DIPOLE_TILT_DEG",\n            "note": "Served so the hover can quote it. The rings are NOT tilted by it. They are drawn in the equatorial plane, which is the daily average of the magnetic one, because a tilted plane needs a direction as well as an angle and that direction turns once a day while this scene is frozen (L-231, Tony\'s ruling 2026-09-15)."\n          },'),
        ('            "in": {"au": {"value": 5e-05, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 7000.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 2.3e-10, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 1.1, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.01, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},\n            "figures": 2,\n            "source": "Meredith, N. P., Horne, R. B., Kersten, T., Fraser, B. J. and Grew, R. S. (2014), \\"Global morphology and spectral properties of EMIC waves derived from CRRES observations\\", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 119, 5328-5342, doi:10.1002/2014JA020064 -- introduction, first paragraph: the inner belt extends from about 1.1 to 2 Earth radii in the geomagnetic equatorial plane.",\n            "source_note": "introduction, first paragraph: the inner belt extends from about 1.1 to 2 Earth radii in the geomagnetic equatorial plane. Open full text at nora.nerc.ac.uk. Meredith states this as background and passes it on from Baker et al. (2007), which is the layer below rather than the source.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_INNER_BELT_INNER_EDGE",\n            "note": "Koskinen and Kilpua (2022) sec. 1.1 corroborate 1.1 to 2 R_E but are NOT the source, because they state it for the inner ELECTRON belt and put the energetic protons over about 1.1 to 3 R_E. The figure matched and the scope did not."\n          },',
         '            "in": {"au": {"value": 5e-05, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 7000.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 2.3e-10, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 1.1, "figures": 2, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.01, "figures": 2, "prints": null}},\n            "figures": 2,\n            "source": "Meredith, N. P., Horne, R. B., Kersten, T., Fraser, B. J. and Grew, R. S. (2014), \\"Global morphology and spectral properties of EMIC waves derived from CRRES observations\\", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 119, 5328-5342, doi:10.1002/2014JA020064 -- introduction, first paragraph: the inner belt extends from about 1.1 to 2 Earth radii in the geomagnetic equatorial plane.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_INNER_BELT_INNER_EDGE",\n            "note": "Koskinen and Kilpua (2022) sec. 1.1 corroborate 1.1 to 2 R_E but are NOT the source, because they state it for the inner ELECTRON belt and put the energetic protons over about 1.1 to 3 R_E. The figure matched and the scope did not."\n          },'),
        ('            "prints": 2,\n            "figures": "exact",\n            "source": "Li, X., Xiang, Z., Mei, Y., O\'Brien, D., Brennan, D., Zhao, H., Baker, D. N. and Temerin, M. A. (2025), \\"A New Electron and Proton Radiation Belt Identified by CIRBE/REPTile-2 Measurements After the Magnetic Super Storm of 10 May 2024\\", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 130, e2024JA033504, doi:10.1029/2024JA033504 -- sec. 1: the outer radiation belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5. This row is the lower end of that band. Li et al. (2015), doi:10.1002/2014JA020777, sec. 1 -- greatest intensity between 4 and 5 equatorial R_E for electrons above 500 keV. Kellerman et al. (2014), as reported in \\"Electron intensity measurements by the Cluster/RAPID/IES instrument in Earth\'s radiation belts and ring current\\" (2018, arXiv:1809.00902) -- maximum electron flux at L = 4-5. The arXiv paper is the document opened; Kellerman is the layer below it. These two corroborate; they were carried on the peak row\'s \\"# Declared:\\" line until 2026-09-22 and were not re-read here.; Li et al. (2025), doi:10.1029/2024JA033504 -- sec. 1: the outer radiation belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5. This row is the upper end of that band. Corroborated as the row above.",\n            "source_note": "sec. 1, the outer belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5; the value served is our midpoint of that band. Kellerman et al. (2014), as reported in arXiv:1809.00902 -- maximum electron flux at L = 4-5; the arXiv paper is the document opened and Kellerman is the layer below it.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_RADII",\n            "note": "A flux PEAK, not an edge. The unit is L, the McIlwain parameter, which equals geocentric distance in Earth radii only where a field line crosses the magnetic equator -- so any string printing this value says \'at the equator\'. Baker et al. (2018) was removed from this citation on 2026-09-14: his figure 30 uses L* = 4.5 as a selected analysis location rather than a universal peak, and his span figure belongs on the edge row below."\n          },',
         '            "prints": 2,\n            "figures": "exact",\n            "source": "Li, X., Xiang, Z., Mei, Y., O\'Brien, D., Brennan, D., Zhao, H., Baker, D. N. and Temerin, M. A. (2025), \\"A New Electron and Proton Radiation Belt Identified by CIRBE/REPTile-2 Measurements After the Magnetic Super Storm of 10 May 2024\\", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 130, e2024JA033504, doi:10.1029/2024JA033504 -- sec. 1: the outer radiation belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5. This row is the lower end of that band. Li et al. (2015), doi:10.1002/2014JA020777, sec. 1 -- greatest intensity between 4 and 5 equatorial R_E for electrons above 500 keV. Kellerman et al. (2014), as reported in \\"Electron intensity measurements by the Cluster/RAPID/IES instrument in Earth\'s radiation belts and ring current\\" (2018, arXiv:1809.00902) -- maximum electron flux at L = 4-5. The arXiv paper is the document opened; Kellerman is the layer below it. These two corroborate; they were carried on the peak row\'s \\"# Declared:\\" line until 2026-09-22 and were not re-read here.; Li et al. (2025), doi:10.1029/2024JA033504 -- sec. 1: the outer radiation belt is most intense around L = 4 and 5. This row is the upper end of that band. Corroborated as the row above.",\n            "source_note": "the value drawn is our midpoint of that band",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_RADII",\n            "note": "A flux PEAK, not an edge. The unit is L, the McIlwain parameter, which equals geocentric distance in Earth radii only where a field line crosses the magnetic equator -- so any string printing this value says \'at the equator\'. Baker et al. (2018) was removed from this citation on 2026-09-14: his figure 30 uses L* = 4.5 as a selected analysis location rather than a universal peak, and his span figure belongs on the edge row below."\n          },'),
        ('            "in": {"au": {"value": 0.0001, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 20000.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 6e-10, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 3.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.03, "figures": 1, "prints": null}},\n            "figures": 1,\n            "source": "Meredith et al. (2014), doi:10.1002/2014JA020064 -- introduction, first paragraph: the outer belt extends from 3 to 7 R_E. Li, Tu, Selesnick and Huang (2024), \\"Modeling the contribution of precipitation loss to a radiation belt electron dropout observed by Van Allen Probes\\", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 129, e2023JA032171, doi:10.1029/2023JA032171 -- introduction, first paragraph, the same span independently.",\n            "source_note": "both state the outer belt extending from 3 Earth radii, in their introductions. Open full text at nora.nerc.ac.uk and par.nsf.gov.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_BELT_INNER_EDGE",\n            "note": "The layer below is Paulikas and Blake (1979) with Baker et al. (1986) for Meredith, and Ganushkina et al. (2011) with Van Allen et al. (1958) for Li. Baker et al. (2018) sec. 2 gives the same inner edge from SAMPEX and reports it reaching L ~ 2.5 under strong driving."\n          },',
         '            "in": {"au": {"value": 0.0001, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "km": {"value": 20000.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "pc": {"value": 6e-10, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "r_earth": {"value": 3.0, "figures": 1, "prints": null}, "r_sun": {"value": 0.03, "figures": 1, "prints": null}},\n            "figures": 1,\n            "source": "Meredith et al. (2014), doi:10.1002/2014JA020064 -- introduction, first paragraph: the outer belt extends from 3 to 7 R_E. Li, Tu, Selesnick and Huang (2024), \\"Modeling the contribution of precipitation loss to a radiation belt electron dropout observed by Van Allen Probes\\", J. Geophys. Res. Space Physics 129, e2023JA032171, doi:10.1029/2023JA032171 -- introduction, first paragraph, the same span independently.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_VAN_ALLEN_OUTER_BELT_INNER_EDGE",\n            "note": "The layer below is Paulikas and Blake (1979) with Baker et al. (1986) for Meredith, and Ganushkina et al. (2011) with Van Allen et al. (1958) for Li. Baker et al. (2018) sec. 2 gives the same inner edge from SAMPEX and reports it reaching L ~ 2.5 under strong driving."\n          },'),
        ('            "Outer Radiation Belt"\n          ],\n          "descriptions": [\n            "A ring of trapped charged particles, mostly protons, held by Earth\'s magnetic field close above the atmosphere.",\n            "A broader ring of trapped electrons farther out, that swells and shrinks with solar storms."\n          ],\n          "abouts": [\n            "The inner Van Allen belt is a region of energetic protons trapped by Earth\'s magnetic field. It follows the magnetic equator, which is tilted from the geographic one and turns with Earth once a day; the ring drawn here lies in the daily average of that plane.",\n            "The outer Van Allen belt is mostly energetic electrons, trapped farther out than the inner belt, and it swells and shrinks as the solar wind changes. Like the inner belt it follows the magnetic equator; the ring drawn is the daily average of that tilted plane."\n          ],\n          "flux_peak_of": [\n            "trapped protons",',
         '            "Outer Radiation Belt"\n          ],\n          "descriptions": [\n            "A belt of trapped charged particles, mostly protons, held by Earth\'s magnetic field close above the atmosphere.",\n            "A broader belt of trapped electrons farther out, that swells and shrinks with solar storms."\n          ],\n          "abouts": [\n            "The inner Van Allen belt is a region of energetic protons trapped by Earth\'s magnetic field. The particles are held along the field lines, so the belt curves down toward the ground on either side of the equator. The real field is tilted from Earth\'s axis and turns with Earth once a day; the belt drawn here follows the field\'s daily average, lined up with the spin axis.",\n            "The outer Van Allen belt is mostly energetic electrons, trapped farther out than the inner belt, and it swells and shrinks as the solar wind changes. Like the inner belt it is held along the field lines, which meet the ground nearer the poles; it is drawn around the field\'s daily average, lined up with the spin axis."\n          ],\n          "flux_peak_of": [\n            "trapped protons",'),
        ('          ],\n          "hovers_band": [\n            "",\n            "Drawn at {drawn} Earth radii: halfway across the band, {low} to {high} Earth radii out at the magnetic equator, where the belt is most intense. The halfway point is our choice for the picture, not a measured peak."\n          ],\n          "hovers_rings": [\n            "The belt is one continuous region; its evenly spaced rings only mark its extent, and the brighter ring marks where it is most intense.",\n            "The belt is one continuous region; its evenly spaced rings only mark its extent, and the brighter ring marks where it is most intense."\n          ],\n          "hovers_plane": [\n            "The ring lies in Earth\'s equatorial plane, the daily average of the magnetic equator, which turns with Earth and in 2020 was tilted {tilt} degrees from it{rate} (IGRF-13 model).",\n            "The ring lies in Earth\'s equatorial plane, the daily average of the magnetic equator, which turns with Earth and in 2020 was tilted {tilt} degrees from it{rate} (IGRF-13 model)."\n          ],\n          "info_urls": [\n            "https://science.nasa.gov/biological-physical/stories/van-allen-belts/",\n            "https://science.nasa.gov/biological-physical/stories/van-allen-belts/"\n          ],\n          "_declared": "n_points, colours, names and info_borders are drawing choices; info_borders mirrors the orrery\'s two-standards outline (shell_configs.py). Each belt is drawn as rings evenly spaced from its served inner edge to its served outer edge, on the step that also lands on its served peak, so the number of rings follows from those rows and is not a setting; the peak ring is drawn brighter and larger. The typed belt_thickness of 0.5 Earth radii and n_rings of 5 were removed rather than promoted (L-322 Stage D, gallery patch 3; the orrery since patch D9).",\n          "planet_radius": {\n            "value": 6378.1366,\n            "unit": "km",',
         '          ],\n          "hovers_band": [\n            "",\n            "Thickest at {drawn} Earth radii: halfway across the band, {low} to {high} Earth radii out at the magnetic equator, where the belt is most intense. The halfway point is our choice for the picture, not a measured peak."\n          ],\n          "hovers_shape": [\n            "The belt is one continuous region. Its dots follow Earth\'s magnetic field lines, which curve from the equator down toward the ground, and they are thickest where the belt is most intense. How they thin out is drawn to show the shape, not measured.",\n            "The belt is one continuous region. Its dots follow Earth\'s magnetic field lines, which curve from the equator down toward the ground, and they are thickest where the belt is most intense. How they thin out is drawn to show the shape, not measured."\n          ],\n          "hovers_plane": [\n            "The field lines are drawn around Earth\'s spin axis: the daily average of the magnetic field, which turns with Earth and in 2020 was tilted {tilt} degrees from it{rate} (IGRF-13 model). The belt stops where its field lines meet the ground.",\n            "The field lines are drawn around Earth\'s spin axis: the daily average of the magnetic field, which turns with Earth and in 2020 was tilted {tilt} degrees from it{rate} (IGRF-13 model). The belt stops where its field lines meet the ground."\n          ],\n          "info_urls": [\n            "https://science.nasa.gov/biological-physical/stories/van-allen-belts/",\n            "https://science.nasa.gov/biological-physical/stories/van-allen-belts/"\n          ],\n          "details": [\n            "Field lines: Koskinen, H. E. J. and Kilpua, E. K. J. (2022), Physics of Earth\'s Radiation Belts, Springer, open access, sec. 1.2.1, The Dipole Field.",\n            "Field lines: Koskinen, H. E. J. and Kilpua, E. K. J. (2022), Physics of Earth\'s Radiation Belts, Springer, open access, sec. 1.2.1, The Dipole Field."\n          ],\n          "drawing": {\n            "_declared": "Not a number: the name of the stored drawing the belts are, data/solar-system/drawings/van_allen_belts.json. The orrery places the dots (van_allen_belt_sampler.py, from the six belt rows above) and the page draws them as given (L-330, Tony\'s rulings of 2026-10-17; the stored-drawing rule of L-421).",\n            "dots": "van_allen_belts"\n          },\n          "_declared": "colours, names and info_borders are drawing choices; info_borders mirrors the orrery\'s two-standards outline (shell_configs.py). Each belt is the stored drawing named under drawing: dots scattered at random along dipole field lines between its served edges, thickest at its served peak, each shaded by its weight. The orrery places them and the page scales them by planet_radius and turns them to Earth\'s pole; how the dots look is a rendering setting in gallery/feature_renderers.js. Until 2026-10-17 each belt was drawn as evenly spaced rings in the equatorial plane (L-322 Stage D, gallery patch 3), and n_points still sets the rings\' points for a body drawn that way. L-330.",\n          "planet_radius": {\n            "value": 6378.1366,\n            "unit": "km",'),
        ('            "n_points": 20,\n            "marker_size": 3.0,\n            "source": "IAU 2012 Resolution B2 -- exact definition; IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, Table 1.1 -- geocentric gravitational constant GM_E = 3.986004418e14 m^3 s^-2 (uncertainty 8e5 m^3 s^-2). Converted to km^3 s^-2 here.; IAU 2015 Resolution B3, published as Prsa et al. (2016), AJ 152, 41, doi:10.3847/0004-6256/152/2/41.",\n            "source_note": "Derived: r_H = a (GM_E / 3 GM_Sun)^(1/3) with a = 1 AU; GM_E from IERS Conventions (2010) TN36 Table 1.1, GM_Sun from IAU 2015 Resolution B3",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_HILL_SPHERE_KM",\n            "note": "About 1.50 million km. The Moon at about 60 Earth radii sits well inside it, which is why it stays bound. a = 1 AU exactly; Earth\'s semi-major axis is 1.00000011 AU (NASA Earth Fact Sheet)."\n          },',
         '            "n_points": 20,\n            "marker_size": 3.0,\n            "source": "IAU 2012 Resolution B2 -- exact definition; IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, Table 1.1 -- geocentric gravitational constant GM_E = 3.986004418e14 m^3 s^-2 (uncertainty 8e5 m^3 s^-2). Converted to km^3 s^-2 here.; IAU 2015 Resolution B3, published as Prsa et al. (2016), AJ 152, 41, doi:10.3847/0004-6256/152/2/41.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_HILL_SPHERE_KM",\n            "note": "About 1.50 million km. The Moon at about 60 Earth radii sits well inside it, which is why it stays bound. a = 1 AU exactly; Earth\'s semi-major axis is 1.00000011 AU (NASA Earth Fact Sheet)."\n          },'),
        ('            }\n          },\n          "rotation_period": {\n            "value": 23.93447,\n            "unit": "hours",\n            "figures": 7,\n            "source": "NIST Special Publication 811, Guide for the Use of the SI, sec. 5.1.1, Table 6 (units the CIPM accepts for use with the SI) -- the hour is 60 minutes, 3600 seconds, by definition.; IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, Table 1.2 \\"Parameters of the Geodetic Reference System GRS80\\" -- nominal mean Earth angular velocity, 7.292115e-5 rad s^-1. Corrected 2026-09-19: the old line said Table 1.1, which has no angular velocity in it. The value is unchanged; only the table is.",\n            "source_note": "Derived in constants_new.py: one full turn divided by the nominal mean Earth angular velocity, 7.292115e-5 rad/s, of IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, Table 1.2 (the GRS80 parameters) -- the sidereal period, one turn measured against the stars.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_SIDEREAL_ROTATION_PERIOD_H"\n          },\n          "source": "The pole the room draws is Earth\'s north pole on the scene\'s date from JPL Horizons (observer quantity 32, target 399), served by the cache builder as pole_of_date. The pole here is the FALLBACK, drawn only when no pole of date is served: the z-axis of the ICRF, which IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, sec. 2.1.1, holds within about 0.02 arcseconds of Earth\'s mean pole at J2000.0, and which the Horizons manual\'s reference frames section describes as aligned with FK5/J2000 to that level. The IAU working group report (Archinal et al. 2018) was cited here until 2026-09-26; it gives no pole for Earth and sends users to the IERS.",',
         '            }\n          },\n          "rotation_period": {\n            "hover_line": "Earth turns once every {value} hours measured against the stars.",\n            "value": 23.93447,\n            "unit": "hours",\n            "figures": 7,\n            "source": "NIST Special Publication 811, Guide for the Use of the SI, sec. 5.1.1, Table 6 (units the CIPM accepts for use with the SI) -- the hour is 60 minutes, 3600 seconds, by definition.; IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, Table 1.2 \\"Parameters of the Geodetic Reference System GRS80\\" -- nominal mean Earth angular velocity, 7.292115e-5 rad s^-1. Corrected 2026-09-19: the old line said Table 1.1, which has no angular velocity in it. The value is unchanged; only the table is.",\n            "orrery_constant": "constants_new.py::EARTH_SIDEREAL_ROTATION_PERIOD_H"\n          },\n          "source": "The pole the room draws is Earth\'s north pole on the scene\'s date from JPL Horizons (observer quantity 32, target 399), served by the cache builder as pole_of_date. The pole here is the FALLBACK, drawn only when no pole of date is served: the z-axis of the ICRF, which IERS Conventions (2010), IERS Technical Note 36, sec. 2.1.1, holds within about 0.02 arcseconds of Earth\'s mean pole at J2000.0, and which the Horizons manual\'s reference frames section describes as aligned with FK5/J2000 to that level. The IAU working group report (Archinal et al. 2018) was cited here until 2026-09-26; it gives no pole for Earth and sends users to the IERS.",'),
        ('              "hover": "The curved arrows at both ends show the sense of the turning: prograde, west to east, counter-clockwise seen from above the north pole.",\n              "source": "Sense of rotation: Archinal et al. (2019), Correction to: Report of the IAU Working Group on Cartographic Coordinates and Rotational Elements: 2015, Cel. Mech. Dyn. Astron. 131:61, doi:10.1007/s10569-019-9925-1 -- Fig. 1 caption: a body whose prime-meridian angle W increases with time rotates prograde. Earth\'s turning: U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, Glossary (consistent with The Astronomical Almanac 2023), https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary -- \'diurnal motion\': the apparent daily motion of the sky from east to west is caused by Earth\'s rotation, so Earth turns west to east; \'direct motion\': counterclockwise as seen from the north pole of the ecliptic, west to east -- the sense Earth\'s turning shares, so it is prograde. The two entries\' wording is the Explanatory Supplement to the Astronomical Almanac\'s (Seidelmann 1992), checked against it 2026-10-06. The 2018 report (Cel. Mech. Dyn. Astron. 130:22) was cited here until 2026-10-06; its sections 2 and 7 were not re-read that day, so the citation moved to what was.",\n              "info_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth%27s_rotation"\n            },\n            "tilt": {\n              "hover": "The axis slowly circles over thousands of years and nods slightly, so the pole and the tilt belong to that date.",',
         '              "hover": "The curved arrows at both ends show the sense of the turning: prograde, west to east, counter-clockwise seen from above the north pole.",\n              "source": "Sense of rotation: Archinal et al. (2019), Correction to: Report of the IAU Working Group on Cartographic Coordinates and Rotational Elements: 2015, Cel. Mech. Dyn. Astron. 131:61, doi:10.1007/s10569-019-9925-1 -- Fig. 1 caption: a body whose prime-meridian angle W increases with time rotates prograde. Earth\'s turning: U.S. Naval Observatory, Astronomical Applications Department, Glossary (consistent with The Astronomical Almanac 2023), https://aa.usno.navy.mil/faq/asa_glossary -- \'diurnal motion\': the apparent daily motion of the sky from east to west is caused by Earth\'s rotation, so Earth turns west to east; \'direct motion\': counterclockwise as seen from the north pole of the ecliptic, west to east -- the sense Earth\'s turning shares, so it is prograde. The two entries\' wording is the Explanatory Supplement to the Astronomical Almanac\'s (Seidelmann 1992), checked against it 2026-10-06. The 2018 report (Cel. Mech. Dyn. Astron. 130:22) was cited here until 2026-10-06; its sections 2 and 7 were not re-read that day, so the citation moved to what was.",\n              "info_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earth%27s_rotation"\n            },\n            "tilt_of_date": {\n              "hover": "Tilt: {tilt} deg on {date}, measured against the orbit of the Earth-Moon barycenter, the gravitational center of the Earth-Moon system, around the Sun that day (JPL Horizons)."\n            },\n            "tilt": {\n              "hover": "The axis slowly circles over thousands of years and nods slightly, so the pole and the tilt belong to that date.",'),
